From 0a4c563d631ef4dd9ac59497ccd7304562482135 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AstroHan <lei.yuhan@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 16:48:19 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(desktop): state a failed turn's outcome where it
 happened
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A failed turn's banner made three separate promises it could not keep.

It claimed an action that does not exist. The banner reads
`describeTurnErrorClass()` for a title and `deriveFailedTurnRecovery()`
for a tail, joined by a CSS `::before` dot, so a rate limit rendered as
「触发模型速率限制 · 已保留部分输出，可从这里继续」. There is no "here"
to continue from: `partialOutputRetained` only records that the turn
produced some assistant text or a tool result, and the sole resume path
is `app_restarted`'s safe resume, gated on `latestInterruptedResumeTurnId`.
`FailedTurnRecoveryAction`'s four values were never read by any renderer
— only `label` was — so the whole recovery layer existed to phrase
guidance no surface could honor. It goes; each `turnError` string now
says what happened and what to do in one sentence, and what it tells the
user to do is send another message, which always works.

It painted its own error surface. `Marker variant="failed-banner"` plus
hand-rolled `oklch(from var(--destructive) …)` borders reimplemented
Astryx's `Banner`, which the same file already uses for the provider
retry indicator and which the app uses in ~50 other places. Reason maps
to `title`, the park diagnostic to `description`, safe resume to
`endContent`. Three `MarkerVariant`s, the bespoke `AlertOctagon`, and
the shell CSS go with it; only the block rhythm stays. Severity follows
the app's existing `tone === 'destructive' ? 'error' : 'warning'` grading
so "restarted, press continue" stops looking as loud as blocked billing.

It rendered above the work it described. The banner sat at the head of
`maka-assistant-answer-content`, before `segment.items.map()`, so an
outcome read as a header on reasoning and tool calls that had all
succeeded. It now follows the timeline it concludes.

Safe resume survives all of this: it is the one recovery with a real
backend, replaying an interrupted turn's own context rather than asking
the user to restate it. Its button and every string around it move from
「安全恢复」 to 「继续这一轮」, so the control, its pending state, its
park description, and its toasts stop describing the same act four ways.

