From c8e78ded56391423889b4a502486892260dfdda5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AstroHan <lei.yuhan@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 19:21:05 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(desktop): keep the side-effect warning a failed turn
 earns
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Deleting `deriveFailedTurnRecovery()` took a real warning with it. A turn
that ran a tool and then failed on transport — `timeout`, `network`,
`rate_limit` — was left with error-class copy alone, so "send a message
to retry" no longer said that tools had already run and a resend could
repeat their effects. That guidance was worth keeping; only the resume
it falsely promised was not.

`describeFailedTurnExecutionState()` restores it as text, with no action
enum to go unread. It does NOT restore the ranking the old derivation
used. That chain put the tool branch ahead of the error class, so `auth`
plus one errored tool advised "inspect the tool result" and dropped
"sign in again" — the only step that could change the outcome — and
`context_overflow` plus tool activity lost "reduce attachments" the same
way. The two facts never competed for real: one is why the turn ended,
the other is what it already did. `Banner` has a slot for each, so the
error class takes `title`, execution state takes `description`, and
neither has to lose. `description` is otherwise written only by the
parked-resume diagnostic, which exists solely on `app_restarted` turns,
so the two cannot collide.

The ordering fix shipped without a test that could fail for it:
`chat-turn-steering-order.test.ts` asserted steering position and
one-occurrence, both true of either layout. It now asserts the banner
follows the last timeline entry, verified by moving the banner back
above the timeline and watching it fail.

